Hmm, wait. Maybe simpler: keep everything in App.tsx. Let me design the files:

**src/theme-setting.ts** — hook:
```ts
import * as React from 'react';
import type {Theme} from '@gravity-ui/uikit';

const STORAGE_KEY = 'gravity-ui-theme';

type SwitchableTheme = 'light' | 'dark';

function getInitialTheme(): SwitchableTheme {
  const stored = window.localStorage.getItem(STORAGE_KEY);
  return stored === 'dark' ? 'dark' : 'light';
}

export function useThemeSetting(): [SwitchableTheme, () => void] {
  const [theme, setTheme] = React.useState<SwitchableTheme>(getInitialTheme);
  React.useEffect(() => {
    window.localStorage.setItem(STORAGE_KEY, theme);
  }, [theme]);
  const toggleTheme = React.useCallback(() => {
    setTheme((prev) => (prev === 'light' ? 'dark' : 'light'));
  }, []);
  return [theme, toggleTheme];
}
```

**src/ThemeSwitcher.tsx** — the component:
```tsx
import {Icon, Switch, Text, Flex} from '@gravity-ui/uikit';
import {Moon, Sun} from '@gravity-ui/icons';

interface ThemeSwitcherProps {
  theme: 'light' | 'dark';
  onToggle: () => void;
}

export function ThemeSwitcher({theme, onToggle}: ThemeSwitcherProps) {
  const isDark = theme === 'dark';
  return (
    <Flex alignItems="center" gap={2}>
      <Icon data={Sun} size={18} />
      <Switch
        checked={isDark}
        onUpdate={onToggle}
        size="l"
        title="Переключить тему"
        aria-label? 
      />
      <Icon data={Moon} size={18} />
      <Text variant="body-2">{isDark ? 'Тёмная' : 'Светлая'}</Text>
    </Flex>
  );
}
```

Check Switch props: `SwitchProps extends ControlProps, DOMProps, QAProps` — ControlProps has `checked`, `onUpdate`, `disabled`, etc. Let me verify ControlProps to make sure `checked`/`onUpdate` exist. uikit ControlProps: `checked?: boolean; defaultChecked?; onUpdate?(checked: boolean); onChange?; onClick?; onFocus?; onBlur?; disabled?; id?; name?; value?; ...` Let me verify quickly via d.ts.

Icon color: icons inherit currentColor; in a row with Sun/Moon I could color active one. Use `color` prop on Icon? Icon has `color` prop? Icon props: `data`, `size`, `width`, `height`, `color`? I recall Icon supports `color` as CSS color string... Let me check Icon.d.ts. I could just leave default (text-primary). To highlight the active icon, could set style color to var(--g-color-text-secondary) for inactive. Keep it simple: both default color; the Switch indicates state. Maybe dim the inactive icon via style={{color: 'var(--g-color-text-secondary)'}}. That's semantic tokens — fine.

**App.tsx** — demo page:
```tsx
import {Button, Card, Flex, Text} from '@gravity-ui/uikit';
import {ThemeSwitcher} from './ThemeSwitcher';
import type {SwitchableTheme} from './theme-setting';

interface AppProps {
  theme: SwitchableTheme;
  onToggleTheme: () => void;
}

export function App({theme, onToggleTheme}: AppProps) {
  return (
    <div className="app" style={{minHeight: '100vh', background: 'var(--g-color-base-background)', color: 'var(--g-color-text-primary)'}}>
      ...
    </div>
  );
}
```

Wait — ThemeProvider non-scoped writes classes to document.body. The `g-root` class on body sets background? In uikit v7, `styles.css` defines `.g-root { background: var(--g-color-base-background); color: var(--g-color-text-primary); }`? Let me check: uikit applies root styles to `.g-root`. Actually I recall `.g-root` sets background and color. Let me verify in styles.css. If body gets g-root_theme_dark class, background follows automatically. Then App doesn't need to set background. But html/body height etc. Let me check what `.g-root` styles do in the installed CSS.

Also check tsconfig strictness, and run typecheck (`npm run build` runs tsc --noEmit && vite build; or just `npx tsc --noEmit`).

Also, main.tsx edit. And check `__agent_eval__` dir — probably eval harness; ignore.

Let me verify: ControlProps, Icon props, .g-root CSS, Text variants (body-2 exists per traps), Card props (theme/type?), Flex props (alignItems, gap, justifyContent?).

